today we're going to be speaking with  Chris feinen who's the executive  director of the National Coalition  against censorship and also author of  the book How Free Speech saved democracy  uh Chris really great having you on I  appreciate it well thanks I appreciate  you having me you know we've had  conversations about the first amendment  that were very legally oriented and  we've had those often with law  professors and others one of the  interesting things about your book is  that you delve more into the history of  when the suppression of speech and the  fight for free speech were you  contextualize it in history in some  interesting ways and one of the things  you point to just to start somewhere and  we could start anywhere you talk about  war as being one of the big culprits  used by governments to suppress free  speech and I think that won't come as a  huge shock to many in our audience when  we understand the idea of for the  purposes of supporting the troops for  National Security whatever don't say  this or say that that I think will make  sense but can you talk about some of the  examples of when war was used in this  way  sure because actually War World War one  was  um civil liberties meltdown in this  country and actually precipitated  lawyers for the first time to start  thinking about using the First Amendment  to protect speech that the government  might not agree with  um you know the the Sedition Act and in  passed by Congress in  um at the behest of President Wilson  actually provided prison sentences of up  to 10 years for people who spoke out  against the war and it was ultimately  used to prosecute over 2 000 Americans  some for the most inane kinds of  criticisms like Wilson is a blockhead  or people who circulated petitions  um you know the most today what we would  say is the most um  uh frequent forms of speech uh were  suppressed  um and uh and people were sent to jail  over a thousand people were sent to jail  many lives were ruined  um and it was against that background  that  um that the ACLU was formed in 1920 and  um and began to fight and of course you  know in subsequent Wars we would see  um similar efforts at suppression  certainly during the Vietnam war  uh what modern Administration and we can  include the Vietnam War era in that I  mean you know in the last 80 or so years  was most harmful I guess is the word I  would use when it comes to suppression  of speech is the is there a clear  Administration I guess  well yeah Wilson clearly stands out  because  there was so uh there was so little law  actually on the First Amendment before  the 20th century and and there had been  a whole Century before and go you know  go all the way back to the Alien and  Sedition Acts right at the turn of the  the 18th century into the 19th  um newspaper editors were thrown into  jail for criticizing the administration  John Adams  um and um  so  um so there was but there was very  little law on uh First Amendment rights  before  um you know before World War one so and  then you know the  um there were certainly efforts during  the the Nixon Administration around the  Pentagon papers efforts to suppress  right  um you know that very critical study of  the Vietnam War  um you know Daniel Ellsberg was actually  indicted for sharing that with the New  York Times And The Washington Post and  um and might well have gone to jail if  the Supreme Court hadn't stepped in and  said that  um that that was protected speech are  you surprised or I guess what do you  make of how the American right wing  recently has very proactively claimed  Free Speech as one of its core tenets  despite sort of not only having a shaky  history on the subject in the past but  even while claiming uh that as one of  its tenants you know talking about Trump  wants to jail flag burners or open up  libel laws which basically is code for  make it easier to sue newspapers and  media outlets for doing journalism uh is  it is it surprising historically that  you would see that uh seeming uh  conflict and what else do you sort of  think about that  well it's wildly ironic yeah it's wildly  ironic to hear conservatives  um advocating as Defenders of free  speech when over the course of American  History they have been the the major  violators of uh of of that freedom  because they've often you know been in  the majority and the people who have  been advocating for free speech have  been people fighting for equal rights  and that was something that you know  they opposed and  um you know this goes back to the  um it goes back  to the abolitionists and to the women's  women's rights activists and activists  for sexual Freedom  um you know they you know they have  um at every of every turn conservatives  have tried to suppress uh suppress their  speech and right through the civil  rights movement of course and into the  into the modern era and as you say  Donald Trump was a big  um uh you know wave the big stick  against the Press called the press the  enemy of the people  um uh so yes to suddenly see  conservatives advocating you know for  free speech rights is um ironic and also  typical for us because it alienates our  kind of our traditional base which is  young people and liberals and  progressives who have been part of those  equal rights fights there are many  accusations hurled at the left about  suppressing speech including uh  different types of alleged censorship on  college campuses both when it comes to  alleged lack of political diversity  among faculty but also as it pertains to  invited speakers to college campuses has  become a very big kind of Flashpoint to  some degree along the lines of LGBT  discussions most recently when it comes  to trans rights the right will sometimes  hurl allegations at the left what do you  think is the biggest area of concern on  the left when it comes to stifling free  speech  well I think you know I think and that  again is you know a change in the the  political climate  um around free speech  but  um  you know I think that it's  understandable  um in a world that's suffused now with  misinformation and hate speech that  um that people progressives  sometimes tried to suppress speech  themselves and we are concerned about  those efforts on campus and we are  concerned about we certainly support the  right to protest speakers on campus and  to  um and to present the other view but we  really think it's it's a mistake to try  to suppress them because they then  conservatives just turn around and point  to that as justification for the the  suppression that they wanted right yeah  my view on this has been you know an  unsavory guest is invited to a campus  someone I'm not interested in seeing  fine  um if they have been properly invited  and there's no criminal issue and  whatever else certainly inter there's  nothing wrong with the invitation and  the fact that they have an event  protesting outside the event absolutely  fine with it the protesters shouldn't be  restricted to some pen a block and a  half away where nobody sees that be  absolutely fine once the event starts it  does seem to me that it's correct to  allow the speaker to speak and then you  can continue expressing your displeasure  during a q a for example the  Counterpoint will be well there might be  no q a so your only opportunity is while  the speaker is speaking or  um I understand that it starts to get to  one of these things of well you know the  protests can't only be the way  everybody's comfortable part of  protesting historically is sometimes the  protests happen in a way and at a time  and in a place where it is an imposition  on some one else but fundamentally I I  do think that preventing the speaker  from speaking is a mistake  yeah and I think it also applies to  trying to revoke invitations that have  been made to speakers if there are  people on campus who want to hear that  speaker they should have the right to to  hear them but by the same token those  who don't have an absolute right to to  protest and  um but we don't want to you know we  don't want to say Free Speech only for  our side which is really what the  conservatives are saying it's it is free  speech for us  um and and not for you because then that  lays the groundwork for what we are  seeing now around the country which is  these hundreds hundreds of  challenges to books in schools and  libraries the progressives care deeply  about there these are books about race  and the Persistence of racism in America  and how to look at slavery and  um uh in the in the lives of LGBT people  and you know their right to be  represented in school libraries and  um and so you know we can't we have to  protect this is the classic statement we  have to protect the rights of all in  order to ensure that everybody has  rights when it comes to the book Banning  you know the the the truth is I have  researched it is that the right is  overwhelmingly the side that has the  lists of books that should not be in  libraries but very often the right will  hurl back at the left look at what  happened with Dr Seuss they canceled Dr  Seuss Etc to me it does not seem  analogous for a publisher to decide  we're not going to continue new editions  of certain books because some of the  imagery or messaging is outdated based  on current Norms that's very different  than here's a list of 20 books that  shouldn't even be available in the  library from your perspective are these  two very different things or are they  equivalent the way the right States  no I I don't think they're equivalent I  I think that um and they're and the  cases are very slim you know the the  cases that they can point to of  alleged suppression of books certainly  um just don't hold up the the  overwhelming majority of challenges to  books in schools are coming from coming  from conservatives and um and parents  who just don't want their kids or  anybody else's kids to have access to  books that in many cases are are  life-saving because they  they show LGBT kids that they belong  that they are accepted  um and these are you know these are very  important books and also how we present  our history  last thing I want to ask you about  um what do you make of the tie between  actual issues of suppression of speech  by governments and some of the sort of  cancel culture social media Private  Business discussion that now is going on  certainly legally I think at this point  most of us although some people call me  and they don't seem to get this  difference most of us understand that  legally uh Twitter can have terms of  service wherein they don't allow certain  types of statements to be made it's not  a First Amendment issue it has nothing  to do with that that we understand so we  can sort of skip beyond that but  ideologically  and from a principles standpoint when  there is not a legal issue what would an  organization like yours say about some  of the terms of service on these social  media platforms that do limit the things  that are allowed  well we understand  um the power of social media and um and  we understand you know why the decisions  that get made on social media are so  um uh are so important to people we have  we have an Arts advocacy project  ourselves to you know try and push back  against algorithms that are banning all  nudity for example including artistic  nudity from from social media  um  uh but ultimately you know we hold with  the view that that the companies have to  be allowed as as is their First  Amendment right to make decisions about  you know what content they allow and and  don't allow and  um uh you know and and unfortunately at  this point you know there are efforts  underway there are laws that have  recently been passed in Texas and  Florida that are trying to force uh  social media companies to accept content  that they don't want right and that you  know that is a step toward government  you know government intervention in uh  free speech that we just can't we can't  allow that we have to challenge yeah  that's an important point it's  ultimately government intervention  either way uh and that's that's a  critical point we've been speaking with  Chris feinen who's the executive  director of the National Coalition  against censorship the book is How Free  Speech saved democracy which we will  link to from our uh YouTube clip for  this interview Chris really appreciate  your time today  thanks for having me

